Z||000003||capt_ron||09-05-2000||11:57 PM||||My 87SE was given to me by a friend who let it sit on the beach for a year. I drove it home but had to replace the tires, distributor, front brakes, and remove the cat. All this cost me ~$600 and I drove it a year before replacing the clutch. It does need a paint job but my girl friend drives it to work everyday since the A/C works so well. I've had this car for over 3 years and have taken several long trips in it. I do all of the work on it myself and by staying on top of the maintenance I have no trouble keeping it running perfectly. <BR>Capt Ron  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||09-06-2000||02:10 AM||||got it running. haven't done much more to it, just tried to start it again and it worked. once i got it started, i did turn the distributor to get it running smoother. still need to time it along with some kind of tune up. also i see a few vaccume lines i need to replace. let it run for about 1/2 hr. seems to run pretty smooth. no over heating, or irregular guage readings (assuming guages are good). shifter feels good rowing through gears. even reverse some may remember i had been worried about that.<P>now i need to get at least the bad tire off. car came with lug locks but no key. no licence yet, so it looks like a few after dark test drives may be in order. <p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 09-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||09-06-2000||08:20 AM||||Philphine,<P>If you haven't looked already, lift up the spare and see if the lock key is there. If no, buy a cheap 12 point socket that is one size too small to easily slide over the lock. Then, get a BFH and drive it on. Use a ratchet or breaker bar to twist it off. Take a punch and drive the old lock out of the socket and move on to the next wheel. I've done this to several parts cars and it works great with no damage to the wheels.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||09-07-2000||11:52 AM||||I just took the 88 coupe that I recently bought down to Goodyear. The guy had the locks off in no time. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||09-09-2000||10:51 PM||||having trouble with my phone line that kept me off line, but mrfixit, that was what i did. used a wheel/tire from my '87 (kept them for spares) to replace bad one. tire not the best but will let me drive it. <P>it is now about a $300 fiero. tried my first test drive and only made it down the street. battery weak and alternator bad (tested at auto zone). some decendant of the marquis de sade must have had some input on the engine bay design. ended up taking the brace that holds the alternator and the oil filter off (so it gets the oil change too).my '87 is much easier. i guess some one knew it had to be redesigned. got dark so i'll try to get it back together tomorrow and try another test drive.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||09-10-2000||09:56 PM||||got it back together. just so you know, don't take the lower alternator bracket off if you can help it. the lower bolt is part of the motor mount. i had a hard time getting it back 'cause the engine dropped a little.<P>took it for a drive. not too bad but one brake caliper started to lock up, then i had no brakes. i'm thinking the flexible hoses at the wheel need replacing. dark when i got home so i'll have to look tomorrow. i think one may have ruptured.<P>holding at $300 for now.||||reg||1
